A suggestion: at the same level in your directory tree where the development of software
occurs; when you are editing source code files and building one or more Apps.  In Arduino
ecosystems the name of the App's root ".ino" file must be the same as the App directory.
This results in a directory of Apps, each with the ".ino" file and a SimpleSal directory.




The structure of this directory matches the structure of the development directory under Apps.  This
is the point of control that interconnects the dirty development directory and the clean repository.
The portal, or the liminal space, between "user source control" and "repository source control" is key.

The term "read-only" means "something blocks the modification of the contents of the file".
The term "writable" means "nothing blocks the ...".

Each directory will contain a read-only or writable copy of a repository's contents, with no Git data.

The repository directory is the possession of the manager and can be changed at any moment.  Modifying
the content of repository directory outside the manager application is only for the most advanced user.

The repo manager uses the file system to enforce the "something/nothing blocks" modification controls.
Carrier and development modification controls are implemented by the user, personally, in their head.

A carrier directory with a read-only copy of a repository's development tree can be referred to directly
by a build path directive.  In the Arduino ecosystem, each App directory contains all the files included;
each App's directory will contain a copy of the carrier's data.  The App copy may be modified but would
never be copied back to the carrier and therefore would never be transferred back to the repository.

A carrier directory with a writable copy can also be referred to directly in a build path directive.
This makes the carrier directory into a development directory.  This also makes the development directory
into the carrier.


